# The White-Robed Leader Thiagaraya
Tamil title: வெள்ளுடை வேந்தர் தியாகராயார்

Speech delivered by Arignar Anna on 3 June 1950 at Sir P. Thiagaraya's memorial day meeting organized by the Triplicane branch of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam.

Beloved Chairman and comrades,

First of all, I convey my happiness to the comrades who arranged such a distinguished observance of Sir P. Thiagaraya's memorial day. I am delighted to see lakhs of people gathered in this cramped place to commemorate our departed great warrior Thiagaraya. Why do you sit amid such discomfort to observe his memorial day? The moment you hear his name, a feeling of pride surges through your hearts. It is because he was the first warrior to rouse fallen Dravidam and give it a new consciousness.

Government spies attend every meeting conducted on behalf of our party. We welcome them. Let the government spies too learn of the noble feeling among us. The intellectual revolution that Sir P. Thiagaraya created in those days has now spread everywhere. This intellectual revolution visible among the people will yield excellent results in the future.

## Had He Wished

Thiagaraya was a great and wealthy man. Had he wished, he could have earned many lakhs through commerce. But he did not consider that important. Had he wished, he could have bought most of Madras for himself. Money then possessed many times its present value; a thousand rupees then was equal to a lakh today. Possessing many lakhs in such a period, he could, had he chosen, have made the whole city of Madras his investment, like Ramnath Goenka. He might have had many people ready to serve him whenever he summoned them. Like so many grandees, petty rulers, merchant princes and zamindars who ate, drank, lived luxuriously, passed their time in amusements and enjoyed pleasure, Thiagaraya too could have lived that way.

Had he wished, he might also have devoted himself to renovating old temples or building marble halls. He had enough money even to create another Palani in the north to rival the Palani in the south. He did not attach great value to any of these. He valued his service to society. He created a great awakening in society and wanted our people to occupy the foremost place in politics. Of his own accord, Thiagaraya abandoned a life of pleasure and began travelling a road filled with stones, thorns, wild streams and dark pits.

## A Drop of Rain for a Withered Crop

Sir Thiagaraya appeared, assumed leadership of the great Dravidian people, guided them towards self-awareness, strove to remove the servitude that had marked their lives, and worked to secure a worthy place for them in society, economics and politics. He laboured day and night to make our foundation strong. Who is the fundamental reason for the growth that the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam enjoys today? It is our Thiagaraya. He was the benefactor who breathed new life into the Dravidian movement.

He understood that we must know the country in which we live to be our own and that it is wrong for us to live here as slaves. He could also perceive our ancient greatness. Like a drop of rain falling upon a withered crop, a pleasant breeze touching a weary body, or medicine destroying a fatal consumption at its root, our warrior Thiagaraya appeared as a source of comfort and renewed life to the Dravidians who were then declining. Today we see the result of his efforts to advance the great Dravidian people.

Our organization now has lakhs of members, more than a thousand workers, hundreds of branches, more than ten newspapers and countless books. No one now believes that fire resides in a Brahmin's right hand. Yet in those days it was said that fire did reside there; today we find such fire in everyone's heart. Thiagaraya did not oppose the British then. The road he followed, service to non-Brahmins, was nevertheless perilous because it was an unrepaired new road that few of that age had travelled or understood. He knew very well that it was strewn with the pits of the Sastri's fury, the curses of the Achari's anger, intrigues and many other troubles. Knowing this fully, he travelled that road and raised the banner of social revolution.

## Dravidian Warrior, Awaken, Rise and March!

Thiagaraya's counsel to the people of this land was that they must not become sacrifices to Brahminism. He said: "Do not accept his mediation in religion, his instruction in education, labour for his elevation in society, or become prey to his intrigue in politics. Dravidian warrior, awaken, rise and march! Make your country your own!"

From that day, Thiagaraya's figure became a symbol of revolution in the South. Today we possess a great force, a great force that opposes Brahminism.

## The Seed He Sowed

The seed he sowed in the field has grown well. We work today beneath the banner of social revolution that he raised then. Dr Nair stood beside him, and together they established a sound battle camp against Brahminism. He explained clearly to the people what Brahminism meant. We understand it well today. For many years we have opposed Brahminism and won many victories. The echo of those victories, however, has created agitation in the opposing camp. The government's concern is merely: under which section can C. N. Annadurai be caught? Can he be caught under Section 152? Under what provision of law can he be arrested? This is not truly an attitude born of justice, but of the government's fear. Its alarm at seeing us is the result of fear. Non-Brahmins once feared Brahmin domination; today it is the Brahmins who are afraid. In truth, however, they have no need to fear.

## Not the Brahmin, but Brahminism

Brahmin friends, do not be afraid. We are not your enemies. It is Brahminism that we oppose, the Brahminism that has entered among you. We grow while opposing it and shall continue to grow. Do not obstruct our growth. Wherever you obstruct it, we shall condemn you forcefully. If you wish to destroy the Brahminism that has remained ineradicable among you for many years and establish a new relationship with us, do so. But do not seek to nourish Brahminism. Rejoice on seeing how far the intellectual revolution created by Thiagaraya has grown among us today.

Fear has taken hold of you because you do not obtain places in schools. For that reason you take Alladi and the orthodox Varadachari with you to court, turn through law books and do all manner of things. Even if you win in law, what will happen? Will that resolve the problem? It will only increase the people's dissatisfaction, not lessen it. If you do not obtain places in schools, the honest course is to ask the government to establish more schools and colleges. Do not instead invoke merit and ability or follow a wrongful path. Those are not ways of resolving the problem, but of enlarging it.

## First, an Intellectual Revolution

Thiagaraya appeared at a time when Dravidian consciousness and the idea of Dravida Nadu had faded and Dravidians everywhere were trapped and disintegrating in lives of suffering. His heart grieved at that painful sight. He entered the field of action to fight fiercely and abolish the degradation. Personally, he lacked nothing. Thiagaraya believed intellectual propaganda more important than other kinds of propaganda and began that righteous struggle even then. We are all delighted to see that struggle victorious today. We take pride in the growth of the Dravidian people and in the place many now occupy in political life.

Thiagaraya did not then enter into religious matters or oppose priestcraft, because he wished first to give Dravidians self-awareness and self-respect. Only after awakening and rousing the people did he wish to enter the religious sphere. He knew clearly what our people needed first and therefore undertook that task first.

In those days, if one asked who was the finest doctor, the answer was Dr Rangachari. Who is called the finest doctor now? Dr Gurusami, and we rejoice to hear it. In those days, Mylapore and Triplicane said that the finest English orator was the Right Honourable Srinivasa Sastri. Today, if one asks who is the finest speaker among the great Dravidian people, everyone names Sir A. Ramaswami Mudaliar. If one asked then for the finest economist, various names would be offered. Today our Sir R. K. Shanmukham is universally praised as such. In Tamil music, S. G. Kittappa was once named the finest singer; now it is M. K. Thyagaraja Bhagavathar. Samanna was once called the king of comedy; today it is our N. S. Krishnan. We have Dr Krishnaswami among us to write English editorials finer than those in The Hindu.

Our society has changed and advanced greatly. Who is responsible for the great progress of the Dravidian people? It is our Thiagaraya. In oratory, song, medicine and economics, and indeed in every field, Dravidians can display their strength. Conditions have changed greatly since Thiagaraya and will change still further. Those who descend beneath the sea and retrieve pearls have always been Dravidians. A pearl we retrieve may adorn an Aryan woman's ear, but the sea remains; so long as the sea exists, pearls will exist, and so long as pearls exist, so shall we. We therefore always possess a future. Aryanism, however, cannot continue as Aryanism once knowledge develops, nor will it remain valued as something rare.

Our movement is the union of the revolutions of Socrates, Voltaire and Lenin. Socrates made fate laugh at the strange orthodox believers. After Socrates, Voltaire assailed the folly of orthodoxy and shattered superstition. Orthodox men ignorant of the world believed it flat; Galileo made them understand it was a sphere and was attacked for doing so. After all these revolutions Rousseau arose and demanded respect for the people's assembly. Wycliffe, Zwingli, Calvin and others condemned scriptural authority and pleasure-seekers who paid money to houses of prostitution. Karl Marx later fought against the cruelty of capitalists, and after Marx, Lenin arose to fight them.

Only after Voltaire, the warrior who used his sharp pen to attack palaces, abodes of ignorance and fortresses of antiquity, had triumphed could the revolutionary Lenin fight and prevail. Only after Voltaire had brought about a revolution in society did the people welcome Lenin.

First Socrates, then Voltaire, and after him Lenin: these three events occurred at different times, all for social revolution. Nor was there any lack of a chain of reform among them: Plato followed Socrates, Rousseau followed Voltaire, and Lenin followed Marx. Thus opposition to superstition, intellectual revolution and social revolution have spread everywhere. In our country, however, our movement must conduct all three together. That is why our revolution is a union of three revolutions.

Only after creating an intellectual revolution among the people can they be reformed in religion, art and culture. Thiagaraya first opened the eyes of our understanding. With that understanding we now oppose religion, break many other superstitious customs and strive to end the country's decline. Our movement today has become one sweeter than a delicacy made by pressing three distinct fruits into one, mixing in sugar and powdered rock candy, pouring in fragrant honey, fresh milk and coconut milk, adding sweet pulses, kneading it with aromatic ghee, and taking it from a gentle heat. Yet the government imposes Sections 144, 124 and 153 upon such a movement.

## To Our Brahmin Friends

When Brahmin domination prevailed, our children could not secure education amid many hardships, and Brahmins used that circumstance to study in greater numbers. Will our Brahmin friends now look at us and say, "Let your students study hereafter; we shall engage in other work"?

Brahmin friend, try saying it and let us see. You will not. Will you say, "We have studied enough for many years. We shall now study the Vedas and Agamas and create renewed value for them in the future"? Is your real fear that the Vedas and Agamas may no longer command respect? Because you do not obtain what you consider a proper place in colleges, you send emissaries to C. R., run in search of Sir Alladi, wonder whether to telegraph Nehru and anxiously approach Patel.

What can you achieve by sending emissaries in this fashion? Go and appeal to the authorities above if you wish. Ask The Hindu to write an editorial entitled "Education and Offices." Tell us, if you please, to take up the plough and irrigation lift as before and engage in rural reconstruction; conduct an agitation for it. No one will prevent you. If you have courage, ask for anything justly and properly, and we shall answer.

## After Us

Among those who exist today, we are the moderates (Moderate). Do you know that? Behind us stands a storm. We restrain that storm and direct it along the proper course. If we are ignored, what comes after us will indeed be a great storm. Brahmin friends must therefore change these old practices.

## Devotion Here, Atomic Power There

Atomic power and still newer forces are being discovered in our day. Western scholars, considering where the waste from atomic energy might be deposited, are studying whether the moon would be suitable. They seek to use the lunar sphere as their refuse bin. Here, however, fanciful stories are told of the moon sporting with celestial maidens. New stories are sought out, invented and turned into objects of devotion. Curious reports appear in the newspapers even today.

One report said that when priests entered the sanctum of the Sabarimala Ayyappan temple, which had been burned by miscreants, and began clearing the ruined objects, they found a large snake, twelve feet long, lying beneath a broken idol. People around it picked up stones to attack it. But P. G. N. Unnithan, president of the Travancore Devaswom Board, approached it gently, patted its hood and cried, "Ayyappa, please go." The snake then moved slowly in the direction he indicated.

Reports like this are published under sensational headlines, boxed and printed in large type. Later a report arrives saying the story was false. That denial, however, does not appear across three columns; it is placed in some corner.

Another newspaper report concerns a holy man in Kodambakkam who was supposedly going to enter samadhi. On his appointed day, he did not leave this world for the next. People had gathered to watch him die, but long after the specified midnight there was still no "disposal of the body." The crowd became angry and rushed at him, asking, "Fraud! You said you would merge with the divine light. Why have you still not attained samadhi?" The holy man poured out evasions: "I said only that I would place my soul upon the Lord, remain motionless and enter silence; I never said I was going to die." The people, enraged after being deluded by the impostor's deceit, turned upon the loincloth-clad ascetic. The situation remained under control only because police came between the people and the holy man.

At a time when the people are acquiring reason, it is not right for newspapers, which ought to be messengers of knowledge, to publish either the story of the Kodambakkam holy man entering samadhi or the story of the snake visiting the Ayyappan temple.

The folly that clouds and subdues people's minds is disappearing; a renaissance is imprinting its mark with gathering speed. Superstition is being defeated throughout the world, and progressive ideas blossom day after day. The responsibility for enriching this climate and preparing its road belongs to journalists. We labour today to abolish religious domination, break superstitious customs and improve the people's economic condition.

## Our Programme, Fulfilled by Them

Whoever the ministers who next sit in the Legislative Assembly may be, they will be our people. We do not seek office. Those in office are and will be our people. It is not necessary that we ourselves take office to carry out the programmes we have advocated for so long. Unexpectedly, others have already been implementing them. It is certain that many more laws will be enacted in this way. The awakening now present among the great Dravidian people will yield great results in the future. We can henceforth expect considerable advancement in politics and economics.

The situation has changed so far that Minister Madhava Menon can speak over All India Radio in support of communal representation. Many Congressmen dressed in khadi and Gandhi caps now speak in its support. Whichever party next enters the legislature, it will perform the work of our organization.

Twenty-five years ago we were stoned for saying that a man should not have more than one wife. What has happened now? The principle has become law. Murugan is exempt because he is a god; if he were a man, the government would punish him and throw him into prison today.

We once stood by the roadside and called for inter-caste marriage. Today you see such marriages taking place in many parts of the country. We demanded property rights for women; today people are fighting for them all the way in Delhi. No one can stop the awakening of our minds. Religious fanatics in Madurai impaled eight thousand Jains because they opposed Saivism. Did that cause the Jains to disappear?

## Knowledge Has Won and Shall Win, Not Repression

The repression the government hurls at us is no great thing. Repression is not merely dangerous; it is a strange force. We are not those who fear it. A party cannot be governed by repression. We shall rescue the people from ignorance and lead human society from the cremation ground called fear to the mansion of life.

Religious fanaticism rewarded Socrates, who proclaimed, "The human world is my god, and service to it is my religion," with a poisoned cup. What perished, however, was religious fanaticism, not the philosophy for which Socrates gave his life. Galileo was kicked and beaten for telling people who did not understand the truth about the world that it was round. What does the world say of such people today? What does it say of Lenin, who destroyed the tyranny of the Tsar and raised the banner of equality?

Do you know what former minister Omandur Ramasamy now says among farmers about the present government? Have you seen Minister Avinashilingam strongly condemn Hindi imperialism? See how greatly conditions have changed.

We are winning many victories. We won a great victory in the Dravida Nadu security-bond case, and now win many victories in the people's forum. This is because our argument possesses integrity. Yes, it is also true that we are skilled in argument. Why conceal that? Skill in argument alone, however, is not the reason for victory. Our causes are just and honest; that is why victory comes to us so readily.

## The Foundation Laid by Thiagaraya

Thiagaraya showed us the road to victory after victory. Just as the world rose after people gathered in dark rooms and wildernesses to hear the teachings of Jesus, Thiagaraya's words slowly entered the huts of the poor throughout the South, then travelled into mansions and created a new awakening in the people's forum.

Sir Thiagaraya demonstrated many innovations. Foremost among them was this non-Brahmin revolution, which brought the Dravidians an extraordinarily great victory.

We very greatly need a collected volume about Thiagaraya. The Triplicane Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam must undertake this worthy task. I request its members to approach Thiagaraya's family and admirers, collect important information about him, and publish a fine volume on Thiagaraya next year. The histories of Sir P. Thiagaraya Chetty and Dr Natesa Mudaliar have still not been written. I lovingly ask our scholar K. Appadurai, who is engaged in Tamil research and service to Tamil, to undertake this work.

I ask you not to stop with gathering at meetings and making speeches, but to engage in constructive work of this kind.

*Editorial note: The book was published in 1985.*

Set about collecting the life of Dr Natesa Mudaliar along with the biographical record of Thiagaraya. The histories of both great men who have departed from us must necessarily be written.

I wholeheartedly praise you for observing so splendidly the memorial day of our departed great warrior, the white-robed leader Thiagaraya. A time will come when the Government of Tamil Nadu itself observes Thiagaraya's memorial day.

Thiagaraya has departed from us, but his memory will never depart. I ask you to go home confident that the Dravidian movement, which follows the principles Sir P. Thiagaraya nurtured through arduous labour and which has grown and risen through them, will win victory after victory. With that request, I conclude my speech.

---NOTES---
“வெள்ளுடை வேந்தர்” is rendered as “the white-robed leader,” preserving Thiagaraya Chetty's well-known public identity and the honorific force of “வேந்தர்.”

“பார்ப்பனீயம்” is translated as “Brahminism,” while “பார்ப்பனரல்லாதார்” is retained historically as “non-Brahmins”; the speech explicitly distinguishes opposition to an ideology from hostility toward Brahmin individuals.

The printed source gives the legal provision in the sequence “144, 124, 153” and separately asks whether Anna could be caught under “152.” These historically specific section numbers are retained without silently harmonizing them.

“Dravida Nadu security-bond case” conservatively renders “திராவிடநாடு - ஜாமீன் வழக்கு,” apparently referring to proceedings requiring a security or bond from the publication; the terse source wording does not identify the formal case title.

The source's asterisked statement that the requested book appeared in 1985 is a later editorial note, not part of Anna's 1950 speech.
